WEST SPRINGFIELD -- Since taking office in January 2016, Mayor William C. Reichelt has made fighting blight a priority of his administration.
You might even say the mayor has declared war on blight, taking the fight to every nook and cranny of this diverse city, whose heavily developed eastern flank gradually gives way to more residential and rural sections to the west and north.
About a year and a half ago, West Springfield had a blight list containing over 100 properties. Today, that list has been cut in half to around 47 properties -- a number that the town's Blight Task Force continues to chip away at.
A new blight ordinance is under consideration by the Town Council, which is tentatively slated to take action on the measure at its Nov. 6 meeting. The ordinance involves registering and monitoring vacant properties before they deteriorate so badly that demolishment or legal action is necessary, according to Kate R. O'Brien, the city's attorney.
Kathy Auer, the keeper of the city's "problem property list" and a member of the Blight Task Force, said the 47 properties range from bankruptcies and foreclosures to estates that were probated.
In some cases, she said, the city works with owners to help them bring their properties into compliance, while other times legal action is taken against the person or financial institution that owns the property.
"Sometimes we bring action against the bank," said Auer, a certified health officer and registered sanitarian with the city's Health Department.
Once blight takes root, it can spread like an aggressive cancer, which is why the Reichelt administration has made it a front-burner issue. The impact is felt the most by neighbors who live near blighted properties, which can hurt real estate values.
The city often plays the role of defender in the war on blight, protecting people from the possibility of fires, vagrants and other troubles, according to Auer. Despite hard-fought victories, whether through the demolition or the renovation and sale of problem properties, "there will always be new ones," she said.
But success can be sweet. Case in point: A former "drug house" was recently taken off the problem list, delighting residents who live near the rundown home. "The neighbors are over the moon," Auer said.
