April 04 --Military investigators continue to probe the lethal Tuesday crash of a Marine helicopter in Imperial County but released few details about the mishap.
Four Marines under the command of 3rd Marine Aircraft Wing died when their CH-53E Super Stallion helicopter crashed around 2:35 p.m. Tuesday about 15 miles west of El Centro, near the desert community of Plaster City and the international border with Mexico.
Marine spokeswoman Capt. Morgan Frazer said that the helicopter had departed the Strategic Expeditionary Landing Field at Marine Corps Air Ground Combat Center Twentynine Palms in San Bernardino County to practice landings in unimproved zones.
"The names of the deceased will be withheld until 24 hours after next-of-kin notification," Frazer said by email.
Frazer declined to name the squadron conducting the training or pinpoint where it is based, saying that these details could prematurely identify the dead crew members.
Although based in Miramar, the 3rd Marine Aircraft Wing oversees units there and at Camp Pendleton, Twentynine Palms and Marine Corps Air Station in Yuma.
"Our thoughts and prayers are with the four U.S. Marines from the 3rd Marine Aircraft Wing who lost their lives in yesterday's Southern California helicopter crash. We pray for their families, and our great... USMC," said President Donald Trump in a Wednesday morning Twitter message.
Located about six miles northwest of town, Naval Air Facility El Centro often hosts Marine crews practicing landings and takeoffs. It's also the winter home of the Navy's aerial acrobatic team, the Blue Angels.
On July 10, a KC-130T airplane crewed by members of the Newburgh, New York -based Marine Aerial Refueler Transport Squadron 452 crashed in rural Mississippi en route to El Centro.
All 16 troops on the aircraft died. They included six Marines and a Navy corpsman from Marine Corps Forces Special Operations Command's elite 2nd Marine Raider Battalion. Also killed was Marine co-pilot Capt. Sean Endecott "Puffin" Elliot. Based in Cherry Point, N.C., Elliot grew up in San Diego County.
Tuesday's helicopter crash occurred on the same day that a Marine AV-8B II jet wrecked in the East African nation of Djibouti. The pilot was listed in stable condition after being treated for injuries incurred after ejecting from the plane.
